## The idea

With **one** kind of bracket a counter is enough: +1 for "(" and −1 for ")". If the counter ever goes below 0, a ")" appeared with nothing open; at the end it must be 0. With **several** kinds a counter is not enough ("([)]" has balanced counts but is wrong), so use a **stack**: push every opener, and every closer must match the opener on top of the stack.

## Step by step

1. One type: open = 0. For each character: "(" → open++. ")" → open−−; if open < 0 → false. Ignore other characters.
2. At the end, balanced only if open == 0.
3. Several types: for an opener ( [ { push it. For a closer ) ] } the stack must not be empty and the popped opener must be its partner; otherwise false.
4. At the end, balanced only if the stack is empty.

## Worked example

**Input:** IsBalancedMultiType("{[()]}") and "([)]"

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| { [ ( | push, push, push → stack { [ ( |
| ) | pop ( → matches → stack { [ |
| ] } | pop [ matches, pop { matches → empty → true |
| "([)]" | push (, push [; then ")" pops [ → does not match → false |

**Result:** "{[()]}" → true; "([)]" → false

## Cost

O(n) time. O(1) memory with one bracket type; O(n) for the stack with several.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public static class ParenCheck {

    // Only ( and ): a counter is enough.
    public static bool IsBalanced(string s) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return true;
        int open = 0;
        foreach (char c in s) {
            if (c == '(') {
                open++;
            } else if (c == ')') {
                open--;
                if (open < 0) return false;    // a ")" with nothing open
            }
            // every other character is ignored
        }
        return open == 0;                       // something left open = unbalanced
    }

    // ( [ { : order matters, so we need a stack.
    public static bool IsBalancedMultiType(string s) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return true;
        Stack<char> stack = new Stack<char>();
        foreach (char c in s) {
            if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') {
                stack.Push(c);
            } else if (c == ')' || c == ']' || c == '}') {
                if (stack.Count == 0) return false;
                char top = stack.Pop();
                if (c == ')' && top != '(') return false;
                if (c == ']' && top != '[') return false;
                if (c == '}' && top != '{') return false;
            }
        }
        return stack.Count == 0;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Null? No parentheses? Long nested input?**

Balanced; balanced; counter is O(1) so depth is free

**Q: Why not a stack?**

One bracket type needs only a depth count — O(1) space

**Q: Shared state?**

None

**Q: A 2 GB string.**

Stream characters; the counter never grows

**Q: Several bracket types.**

Then a stack is needed; say why the counter fails ([)]
